From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 08 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: socfpga: de10nano: UIO doorbell nodes for
 f2h_irq8..15

Adds eight interrupt-only UIO nodes to the MiSTer DE10-Nano DTS, one per
FPGA-to-HPS interrupt line f2h_irq8..f2h_irq15 (GIC SPI 48..55), so that
userspace can block on an FPGA event instead of polling for it.

Rationale: ADR-002-interim, Main_MiSTer rewrite plan
----------------------------------------------------
Main_MiSTer's core loop finds out about FPGA-side events by reading the
cause register over the lightweight HPS-to-FPGA bridge in a spin.  The
interim decision (ADR-002-interim) keeps that register access in
userspace -- the register-window story belongs to the later HAL work --
but replaces the poll with a blocking read() on /dev/uioN.  The kernel
side of that is nothing more than a platform device per doorbell line
bound to drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c, which is what these nodes are.

GIC SPI allocation map
----------------------
The Cyclone V routes its 64 FPGA-to-HPS interrupt lines onto GIC SPI
40..103.  This patch fixes the split:

  SPI 40        f2h_irq0       HDMI_TX_VS, the vsync pulse.  Owned by
                               the MiSTer_fb fbdev driver (patch 0001,
                               node added by patch 0004).  Driven by
                               stock gateware.  Explicitly NOT in the
                               pool: it is a kernel-internal interrupt
                               and handing it to userspace would break
                               the framebuffer.
  SPI 41        f2h_irq1       video_sync, the stock frame-sync pulse.
                               Also driven by stock gateware, ~60 Hz.
                               NOT in the pool -- see the renumber note
                               below.  No node.
  SPI 42..47    f2h_irq2..7    platform reserve, held back.  Named
                               claimants: audio ring watermark, CEC.
                               No nodes; unallocated.
  SPI 48..55    f2h_irq8..15   general doorbell pool.  These eight nodes.
  SPI 56..103   f2h_irq16..63  reserved.  No nodes; unallocated.

Every "SPI nn" above is an SPI *index* -- the value the second interrupt
cell carries, which arm,gic.yaml defines as being "in the range [0-987]"
-- and not a GIC INTID.  INTID = cell + 32, so this pool is INTID 80..87.
The distinction is worth stating because the textbook rule "DT cell =
SPI - 32" is about INTIDs: applying it to the labels above would emit
cells 16..23, which are HPS peripheral interrupts and not f2h lines at
all.  The MiSTer_fb node uses the same convention already ("SPI 40" =
cell 40 = f2h_irq0).

Pool renumber: 41..48 became 48..55 (2026-08-14)
------------------------------------------------
The first version of this patch put the pool at SPI 41..48, on the
premise that SPI 40 was the only f2h line stock gateware drives.  That
premise is wrong, and this revision is the correction.

The stock FPGA wrapper drives the two low lines together:

  f2h_irq = {video_sync, HDMI_TX_VS}          (sys_top.v:573)

so SPI 41 carries a live signal, and a UIO node claimed it.  Measured on
silicon: the node on SPI 41 saw a steady 60.17 Hz of events with re-arm
proven live between them, while the other seven pool nodes saw zero
events and a zero initial event count across 42 s of armed polling.  The
original reading of that run ("eight lines green") counted eight probe
processes exiting 0, not eight firing lines; the per-line asymmetry was
only visible once the event counters were read per node.

Two defects, of different severity, followed from the overlap:

  - ~60 Hz of spurious doorbell traffic on a pool line.  Each frame cost
    a wakeup, a read() and an ack for an event no core raised.
    Recoverable in userspace (an empty cause word drains as "no cause"),
    but pure waste on a latency path, and noise in exactly the counters
    the doorbell work exists to measure.
  - The claim is exclusive and permanent, which is the worse half.
    uio_pdrv_genirq cannot co-own an interrupt at all: its probe fails
    with "interrupt configuration error" if IRQF_SHARED is set, and a
    DT-probed node sets no irq_flags, so the request is unshared by
    construction (uio_pdrv_genirq_probe()).  The node therefore denied
    SPI 41 to every future in-kernel consumer of that line, silently.

Of the two available fixes -- shift the pool by one to 42..49, or
realign it to the 8..15 window the rewrite plan's line-allocation table
reserves -- this takes the second.  The plan of record is the plan of
record, the pool was never this patch's to place, and 48..55 leaves the
42..47 platform reserve contiguous and intact.  The cost of moving is
nil: no gateware drives any pool line today, and no shipping userspace
consumes these nodes.

The node NAMES do not move with the numbers.  They stay
mister_doorbell1..8 because they are ABI (below), so after the renumber
the name carries a pool ORDINAL and not a line number:

  pool ordinal N == f2h_irq(N+7) == DT cell (N+47) == GIC INTID (N+79)

i.e. mister_doorbell1 is f2h_irq8 on SPI index 48 (INTID 80), and
mister_doorbell8 is f2h_irq15 on SPI index 55 (INTID 87).  The trailing
comment on each interrupts property is where the f2h index and the DT
cell are written side by side; keep it in sync with the cell.

Level, not edge
---------------
The doorbell nodes use IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH while the MiSTer_fb node two
lines above uses IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING.  That asymmetry is deliberate and
load-bearing, not an oversight:

  - A doorbell is backed by a cause register the FPGA holds asserted
    until userspace acknowledges it.  With an edge trigger, a second
    event raised while the first is still unacknowledged produces no new
    edge, so it coalesces into the already-high level and is lost with no
    diagnostic anywhere.  Level-high cannot lose it: the line stays
    asserted and re-fires the moment the handler re-enables it.
  - vsync (SPI 40) is the opposite: a true pulse with no cause register
    to read back, so edge-rising is correct there.

uio_pdrv_genirq already handles the level case properly -- on a
level-typed IRQ it sets IRQ_DISABLE_UNLAZY so the line is genuinely
masked while userspace clears the cause register, instead of the lazy
mask re-firing immediately (drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c,
uio_pdrv_genirq_probe()).

A level-high node on a line nothing drives is quiet, not noisy: the
seven pool nodes that sat on undriven lines in the run above recorded no
events at all, and no initial latched count either.  Declaring the pool
ahead of the gateware that will drive it costs nothing.

No reg property
---------------
These are interrupt-only nodes.  uio_pdrv_genirq's resource loop simply
finds no IORESOURCE_MEM and zeroes every uio_mem, so a node with no reg
probes fine and exports no maps.  The reg-bearing UIO nodes are a
separate family, added by patch 0044: memory there, interrupts here.
Keeping the two orthogonal means either family can gain properties
without disturbing the other.

Node names are ABI
------------------
No "linux,uio-name" property is set, so uio_pdrv_genirq derives
uio_info.name from the fwnode ("%pfwP") and userspace identifies a
doorbell by /sys/class/uio/uioN/name == "mister_doorbellN".  Renaming a
node, or giving it a unit address, changes that string.  This is also
why the renumber left the names alone: the minor number a node lands on
already shifts whenever nodes are added, so name is the only stable
handle, and the renumber had no reason to touch it.

This resolves OPEN-4 in the doorbell spec (wavep-doorbell-spec-draft.md
section 8, "DTS node naming scheme after the 0043 pool renumber") in
favour of keeping the names, i.e. making them pool ordinals.  State the
alternative honestly rather than as a non-option: renaming to
mister_doorbell8..15 today would cost exactly one constant, because the
only consumers are the hwpass harness and the greenfield name constant
at MMIO-D3's designated single change point -- and it is never cheaper
than it is now.  The trade taken here is ABI stability over a numbering
scheme that reads directly, accepting the ordinal-to-line indirection as
permanent.  The register entry should be closed with that rationale, or
overruled while the rename is still one constant.

Why this patch is beta-local
----------------------------
This file lives in board/mister/de10nano/linux-patches-beta/ and is
listed only in that directory's series file, so only the 7.2 beta build
applies it.  It is deliberately absent from the shared
board/mister/de10nano/linux-patches/ set, because that directory has no
series file: the stock 6.18 build applies every patch in it via
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_PATCH, and a shared copy would therefore reach the
stock kernel's DTS as well.  Keeping it beta-local is what lets the
stock build stay byte-identical to what it was before any of this work,
which is the property the doorbell series is required to preserve.

Binding on the RT line needs a command-line parameter
-----------------------------------------------------
uio_pdrv_genirq's of_match_table starts empty and is filled in by the
of_id module parameter (module_param_string(of_id, ...)).  Built as a
module that is a modprobe argument; built in, as on the RT kernel
(CONFIG_UIO_PDRV_GENIRQ=y), it has to come from the kernel command line.
board/mister/de10nano/linux-rt.fragment supplies all three halves of
this: CONFIG_UIO=y, CONFIG_UIO_PDRV_GENIRQ=y, and
CONFIG_CMDLINE="uio_pdrv_genirq.of_id=generic-uio" with
CONFIG_CMDLINE_EXTEND=y so it appends to U-Boot's /chosen bootargs
rather than replacing them.

Provenance
----------
Origin:       None.  Originally authored for this tree; no MiSTer fork
              commit corresponds to it (stock MiSTer has no UIO nodes and
              no UIO support in its kernel config).
Author:       Michael C. Ferguson, 2026-08-08.
Revised:      2026-08-14 -- doorbell pool renumbered from GIC SPI 41..48
              to 48..55 after the SPI-41 overlap with the stock
              video_sync line was measured on hardware.  Node names,
              trigger type and node shape are unchanged.
Upstream:     No, and not upstreamable as-is.  "generic-uio" is not a
              documented device-tree binding -- it is a match string
              invented per-system and injected via uio_pdrv_genirq.of_id,
              which is precisely why it must be paired with the cmdline
              parameter.  Disposition "carry" (docs/patch-provenance.md
              3.1).  Anchors on the DTS as patch 0004 leaves it.

Depends on 0004-dts-de10nano-MiSTer.patch (it inserts after the
MiSTer_fb node that patch creates) and must sort after it.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/intel/socfpga/socfpga_cyclone5_de10nano.dts | 151 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 151 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/intel/socfpga/socfpga_cyclone5_de10nano.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/intel/socfpga/socfpga_cyclone5_de10nano.dts
@@ -129,6 +129,157 @@
 		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
 		interrupts = <0 40 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * FPGA-to-HPS doorbell interrupts, exported to userspace via UIO.
+	 *
+	 * ADR-002-interim (Main_MiSTer rewrite plan): Main_MiSTer's core loop
+	 * currently discovers FPGA-side events by polling the cause register
+	 * over the lightweight bridge.  The interim decision is to keep the
+	 * register access in userspace but replace the poll with a blocking
+	 * read() on /dev/uioN, so the FPGA can wake the process instead of
+	 * being asked.  These nodes are the device-tree half of that; they
+	 * carry the IRQ lines and nothing else.
+	 *
+	 * GIC SPI ALLOCATION MAP -- Cyclone V f2h_irq[] (SoC docs: the 64
+	 * FPGA-to-HPS interrupt lines land on GIC SPI 40..103):
+	 *
+	 *   SPI 40        f2h_irq0       HDMI_TX_VS, the vsync pulse.  Owned
+	 *                                by the MiSTer_fb fbdev driver (node
+	 *                                above; patch 0001).  DRIVEN by stock
+	 *                                gateware.  NOT part of the doorbell
+	 *                                pool -- do not add a UIO node for it.
+	 *   SPI 41        f2h_irq1       video_sync, the stock frame-sync
+	 *                                pulse.  Also DRIVEN by stock
+	 *                                gateware, ~60 Hz.  NOT free, NOT part
+	 *                                of the pool, and no node -- see the
+	 *                                renumber note below.
+	 *   SPI 42..47    f2h_irq2..7    platform reserve, held back (named
+	 *                                claimants: audio ring watermark,
+	 *                                CEC).  Unallocated, no nodes.
+	 *   SPI 48..55    f2h_irq8..15   general doorbell pool (these nodes).
+	 *   SPI 56..103   f2h_irq16..63  reserved.  Unallocated, no nodes.
+	 *
+	 * NUMBERING: every "SPI nn" above is an SPI *index*, which is exactly
+	 * what the second interrupt cell below holds (arm,gic.yaml: "SPI
+	 * interrupts are in the range [0-987]").  It is NOT the GIC INTID:
+	 * INTID = cell + 32, so this pool is INTID 80..87.  Do not "correct"
+	 * these cells by subtracting 32 -- cells 16..23 are HPS peripheral
+	 * interrupts, not f2h lines at all, and a node pointed at them is a
+	 * silent brick.  The MiSTer_fb node above already uses this same
+	 * convention ("SPI 40" = cell 40 = f2h_irq0).
+	 *
+	 * WHY 48..55 AND NOT 41..48 (renumbered 2026-08-14): the original
+	 * split assumed SPI 40 was the only line stock gateware drives.  It is
+	 * not.  The stock FPGA wrapper drives BOTH low lines --
+	 * f2h_irq = {video_sync, HDMI_TX_VS} (sys_top.v:573) -- and a UIO node
+	 * on SPI 41 therefore sat on a live ~60 Hz source: measured on
+	 * silicon, that node logged 60.17 Hz of events while the other seven
+	 * pool nodes logged zero across 42 s of armed polling.  Two distinct
+	 * defects came with it.  First, ~60 Hz of spurious doorbell wakes on a
+	 * pool line, each one a wakeup, a read() and an ack for an event no
+	 * core raised.  Second, and worse, the claim is EXCLUSIVE and
+	 * permanent: uio_pdrv_genirq refuses to share an IRQ at all (its probe
+	 * fails with "interrupt configuration error" when IRQF_SHARED is set,
+	 * and a DT-probed node sets no irq_flags), so the node denied SPI 41
+	 * to every future in-kernel consumer of that line.  A doorbell pool
+	 * must sit only on lines no other agent drives; 48..55 is the split
+	 * the rewrite plan's line-allocation table reserves for it, and it
+	 * leaves the 42..47 platform reserve contiguous and intact.
+	 *
+	 * The node NAMES did not move with the numbers.  mister_doorbell1..8
+	 * are pool ORDINALS, not line numbers, and they are ABI (below).  The
+	 * mapping is: pool ordinal N == f2h_irq(N+7) == DT cell (N+47) ==
+	 * GIC INTID (N+79).  The ordinal is what userspace resolves by name;
+	 * the line number is what a core declares in its IRQ: token.  Keep the
+	 * trailing f2h_irqNN comment on each interrupts property in sync -- it
+	 * is where the f2h index and the DT cell are written side by side.
+	 *
+	 * IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH is deliberate, and is NOT a copy-paste slip from
+	 * MiSTer_fb's IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING above.  A doorbell is a cause
+	 * register the FPGA holds asserted until userspace acknowledges it,
+	 * so level is the semantically correct trigger: with an edge trigger,
+	 * a second event raised while the first is still unacknowledged
+	 * produces no new edge and is silently coalesced away.  vsync is the
+	 * opposite case -- a true pulse with no cause register -- which is why
+	 * SPI 40 is edge-rising and these are not.  uio_pdrv_genirq knows the
+	 * difference: for a level-typed IRQ it sets IRQ_DISABLE_UNLAZY, so the
+	 * line is really masked while userspace clears the cause register
+	 * rather than lazily re-firing (drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c).
+	 *
+	 * A LEVEL_HIGH node on an undriven line is quiet, not noisy: the seven
+	 * pool lines that stock gateware ties low recorded zero events and a
+	 * zero initial event count.  Nodes may therefore be declared ahead of
+	 * the gateware that will drive them.
+	 *
+	 * No "reg" on purpose: these are interrupt-only nodes.
+	 * uio_pdrv_genirq accepts zero memory resources (its resource loop
+	 * simply finds no IORESOURCE_MEM and zeroes every map), and the
+	 * reg-bearing UIO nodes are a separate family added by patch 0044 --
+	 * memory there, interrupts here, deliberately orthogonal.
+	 *
+	 * The node NAME is the ABI: uio_pdrv_genirq has no "linux,uio-name"
+	 * property here, so it derives uio_info.name from the fwnode ("%pfwP")
+	 * and userspace matches on /sys/class/uio/uioN/name.  Do not rename
+	 * these nodes, and do not add unit addresses to them.
+	 *
+	 * Binding: the driver's of_match_table is empty until the kernel
+	 * command line fills it, because it is built in on the RT kernel
+	 * (CONFIG_UIO_PDRV_GENIRQ=y) rather than modprobe'd with a parameter.
+	 * board/mister/de10nano/linux-rt.fragment supplies both that symbol
+	 * and "uio_pdrv_genirq.of_id=generic-uio" via CONFIG_CMDLINE_EXTEND.
+	 *
+	 * This patch is applied only by the 7.2 beta series; the stock 6.18
+	 * build never sees it, so the DTS it builds carries no UIO nodes at
+	 * all.
+	 */
+	mister_doorbell1 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 48 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq8 */
+	};
+
+	mister_doorbell2 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 49 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq9 */
+	};
+
+	mister_doorbell3 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 50 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq10 */
+	};
+
+	mister_doorbell4 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 51 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq11 */
+	};
+
+	mister_doorbell5 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 52 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq12 */
+	};
+
+	mister_doorbell6 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 53 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq13 */
+	};
+
+	mister_doorbell7 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 54 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq14 */
+	};
+
+	mister_doorbell8 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
+		interrupts = <0 55 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq15 */
+	};
 };
 
